# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Arrays", worked example "Writing at one position and reading at another".
id: array-aliasing
title: Writing at one position and reading at another
topic: Arrays
level: 3
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Arrays, "Writing at one position and reading at another"
tags: arrays, array assignment, aliasing, select-store

task:
  Find the weakest precondition with the rule ARRAY-ASSIGN. Before
  computing: is there a situation in which the postcondition holds for
  free? What happens if $i = j$? And if $i != j$, what must be true about
  $a[j]$?

pre: (j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)
post: a[i] = a[j]

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  a[i] := 2 * a[j]
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Substitution
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
focus: a[i] :=
text:
  $I$ is $i$ and $E$ is $2 * a[j]$. The postcondition reads the array
  twice, and both reads now refer to the updated array.
calc:
  (a[i] = a[j])[a <- a[i |-> 2 * a[j]]]
  = a[i |-> 2 * a[j]][i] = a[i |-> 2 * a[j]][j]
note:
  The naive rule would only rewrite the cell $a[i]$ and leave $a[j]$
  unchanged. That is wrong whenever $i = j$.

step: Select-store
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN, LOGIC
text:
  The left read is at $i$, the written position, so it gives $2 * a[j]$.
  The right read is at $j$, which may or may not be equal to $i$, so we
  keep both cases.
calc:
  a[i |-> 2 * a[j]][i] = a[i |-> 2 * a[j]][j]
  = (j = i && 2 * a[j] = 2 * a[j]) || (j != i && 2 * a[j] = a[j])
  = j = i || (j != i && 2 * a[j] = a[j])      // A && true is A
  = j = i || (j != i && a[j] = 0)             // only 0 is equal to its double
  = j = i || a[j] = 0                         // A || (!A && B) is A || B

step: Definedness
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN
text:
  The write is at $i$, and $E$ reads $a[j]$. The brackets matter: the
  definedness conditions must hold in both cases of the disjunction.
calc:
  wp(a[i] := 2 * a[j], Q)
  = (j = i || a[j] = 0) && def(a[i]) && def(a[j])
  = (j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)
set: R1 = (j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)
vc: P ==> R1

step: Test with concrete values
rule: LOGIC
text:
  We start each time from the array $4, 0, 9$.

  | $i$ | $j$ | $j = i || a[j] = 0$ | value written | array after | $a[i] = a[j]$ |
  |---|---|---|---|---|---|
  | $0$ | $0$ | true, since $j = i$ | $8$ | $8, 0, 9$ | $8 = 8$, true |
  | $0$ | $1$ | true, since $a[1] = 0$ | $0$ | $0, 0, 9$ | $0 = 0$, true |
  | $0$ | $2$ | false | $18$ | $18, 0, 9$ | $18 = 9$, false |
text:
  The third row shows that the condition $j = i || a[j] = 0$ cannot be
  dropped: with only the definedness conditions, the triple fails.
vc: {0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)} a[i] := 2 * a[j] {a[i] = a[j]}
status: fails
counterexample: a = [4, 0, 9], i = 0, j = 2
result: The weakest precondition is $(j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)$.
